# .NET Framework → .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

## Critical Breaking Changes

| Issue | Why it breaks in .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — removed entirely |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Still exists but unreliable; doesn't interrupt `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` |
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | Race condition: multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose signals, processing only one item |
| Raw `Thread` usage | Works, but `Task`-based patterns are idiomatic and integrate with async ecosystem |

## Race Condition in the Original Code

```
Thread A: Enqueue("a")  → Set()
Thread B: Enqueue("b")  → Set()  (signal already set — no-op)
Worker:   WaitOne() → Reset() → Dequeue("a") → Handle("a")
Worker:   WaitOne() → BLOCKS FOREVER — "b" is stuck in queue
```

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
        {
            Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Failed to enqueue item.");
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signals no more items
        _cts?.Cancel();                      // cancels if items are still pending
        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            try { await _workerTask.WaitAsync(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); }
            catch (TimeoutException) { /* log: worker didn't drain in time */ }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) { /* expected on cancellation */ }
        }
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) => Console.WriteLine($"Processing: {item}");
}
```

## Key Migration Decisions

| Old Pattern | New Pattern | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<T>` | Thread-safe, backpressure-aware, no lost signals |
| `Thread` | `Task.Run` | Integrates with `async/await`, uses thread pool |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | The only supported cooperative cancellation in .NET 8 |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Gracefully terminates `ReadAllAsync` |
| `IDisposable` | `IAsyncDisposable` | Allows `await`ing worker shutdown |

## If You Need Synchronous-Only API

Replace `IAsyncDisposable` with a synchronous `Stop()`:

```csharp
public void Stop()
{
    _channel.Writer.Complete();
    _cts?.Cancel();
    try { _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); }
    catch (AggregateException) { /* handle cancellation */ }
    _cts?.Dispose();
}
```

## Watch Out For

1. **`Handle()` exceptions** — an unhandled throw inside `ReadAllAsync` kills the worker silently. Wrap the loop body in `try/catch` if `Handle` can fail.
2. **Unbounded channels** — `CreateUnbounded` has no backpressure. Use `CreateBounded<string>(capacity)` if producers can outpace the consumer.
3. **Graceful vs. immediate stop** — calling `Complete()` without cancellation drains remaining items; adding `Cancel()` stops immediately. Choose based on your semantics.
